<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#savannah">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Savannah</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Savannah is a type of tropical grassland with scattered trees, found widely across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> between the rainforest and the desert. Its open terrain favoured cavalry warfare, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> herding and grain farming. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire</a>'s cavalry power was based on the savannah, and the term also appears in the profiles of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaabu-empire">Kaabu Empire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rozvi-empire">Rozvi Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/savannah.jpg" alt="Savannah" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ProfessorX · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tarangire-Natpark800600.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Alodia lay in the <b>savannah</b> belt, which gave it an advantage over Makuria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alodia">Alodia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Chancellorsville in April 1863 was followed by the loss of Vicksburg and Port Hudson and the repulse at Gettysburg in July, then Chattanooga; by early 1864 the government controlled 53 per cent of its population, and Sherman's march to <b>Savannah</b> and Grant's siege of Petersburg followed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/confederate-states-of-america">Confederate States of America · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The ecological diversity of the region, combining <b>savannah</b>, riverine and forest zones, supported both agriculture and military mobilisation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kaabu-empire">Kaabu Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>At its height the kingdom covered the Northern, Upper West, Upper East, <b>Savannah</b> and North East regions of present-day Ghana and parts of Burkina Faso, north-eastern Ivory Coast and north-western Togo, and Islam entered northern Ghana through its court and the Dyula and Hausa merchants it welcomed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-dagbon">Kingdom of Dagbon · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The shift of the royal centre to Meroë, completed by around 300 BCE, marked an economic transformation, as the region, with <b>savannah</b> grazing and rain-fed farming beyond the river, became a major centre of iron production fuelled by abundant wood for charcoal; its slag heaps remain visible today.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kushite-empire">Kushite Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Oyo Empire originated in the <b>savannah</b> woodlands of northern Yorubaland, north of the dense rainforest zone that constrained cavalry-based state formation across much of coastal West Africa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
